{:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141859"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-51431"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/2dbcf9be5f2a9ffe89bb0d8601e5be931619bbda9647ce5cf285365085183e74.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-2,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\".\n\nWe can see that the function is curved upward like a smile ($⌣$) over the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/1c5e36abaafab3c3984edd11d99d40ded2b81c8c8e251678e6f617ca522da16c.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-142068/selection/b31e00782a0d8a0a414a995cae2c860a700ece0e30725f0981808172c3bd79f8" :answer/id #uuid "58ec77df-7714-53d1-8722-83ffc6b2bbf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142068/selection/93b236c9f6390974519135b3a74a68a187221ca65303f87151813c65429520b6" :answer/id #uuid "241e11d9-6bf8-5eeb-b124-fd55a9a69563" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=d" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142068/selection/917747ed806fd495b0bd48fa7c96466a148cf287f98ed26a585cb1c448274432" :answer/id #uuid "6f1f551c-05cd-53a1-8b23-d4e1a52c0bbd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142068/selection/f7a3c3073fd18240f3ef35ac5cf3afc829874542a015d352c8124afbc192acb3" :answer/id #uuid "a0a61ead-022e-543e-adff-08c37c425d5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=a" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142068/selection/3e72be51e460f63fb53d45bcdf386a238f4fbffdd5f4e90828eb20f5d4414e2c" :answer/id #uuid "2ee5e8e6-9a48-514b-840f-b19cd2b54709" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142068/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac3b336d-c69f-58e6-8e97-47efc7d31766" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-142068/selection/b31e00782a0d8a0a414a995cae2c860a700ece0e30725f0981808172c3bd79f8" :answer-field/choices ["q-142068/selection/b31e00782a0d8a0a414a995cae2c860a700ece0e30725f0981808172c3bd79f8" "q-142068/selection/93b236c9f6390974519135b3a74a68a187221ca65303f87151813c65429520b6" "q-142068/selection/917747ed806fd495b0bd48fa7c96466a148cf287f98ed26a585cb1c448274432" "q-142068/selection/f7a3c3073fd18240f3ef35ac5cf3afc829874542a015d352c8124afbc192acb3" "q-142068/selection/3e72be51e460f63fb53d45bcdf386a238f4fbffdd5f4e90828eb20f5d4414e2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-142068" :question/id #uuid "f961413e-5822-575e-b3a8-c72a1388167f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142068" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/d8d2a012db4de4a2d9a008bae9d7dae854eb6071e0c5da6d1e65eea6b93f8c97.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)>0?$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is positive when $f(x)$ is increasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=c$ are on increasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/be95fe97afc8c90a372176e3dcf19fe49493d0e92059e7ceb5a4a40e77f23ee6.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=c,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=b$ only." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-142068/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-142068"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-142031/selection/917747ed806fd495b0bd48fa7c96466a148cf287f98ed26a585cb1c448274432" :answer/id #uuid "4b1d8d6f-353a-5b94-bdb8-ed77947042ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142031/selection/3e72be51e460f63fb53d45bcdf386a238f4fbffdd5f4e90828eb20f5d4414e2c" :answer/id #uuid "18a9afea-33ad-5f86-b4aa-a3d1d85e5de8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142031/selection/f7a3c3073fd18240f3ef35ac5cf3afc829874542a015d352c8124afbc192acb3" :answer/id #uuid "0aac7f92-59b7-500e-b7a0-d85ef671e132" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=a" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142031/selection/93b236c9f6390974519135b3a74a68a187221ca65303f87151813c65429520b6" :answer/id #uuid "d403c688-5019-5df4-b8ad-08d622b08a2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=d" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142031/selection/b31e00782a0d8a0a414a995cae2c860a700ece0e30725f0981808172c3bd79f8" :answer/id #uuid "fc7f7d0d-d501-5608-8143-3332220b5609" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-142031/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "39da3983-9428-5424-8cc6-8db82c82fd30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :a